18:56:23 [ecd8] --- STYLE REFERENCE (from the curated 4-to-7 corpus, WHO/WHAT/WHERE subset for this age) ---
18:56:23 [ecd8] Follow the general style, length, vocabulary, and structure of these examples. Each story is followed by 5–7 WH-questions whose answers are explicitly stated in the story.
18:56:23 [ecd8] Example 1 (setting: farm):
18:56:23 [ecd8]   Story: The cows live behind a fence on the farm. They like to walk and eat grass. One day a cow sees some flowers. She wants to taste the flowers! The cow finds an opening in the fence. She pulls a flower out of the basket and chews it.
18:56:23 [ecd8]   Questions:
18:56:23 [ecd8]   - WHERE do the cows live?
18:56:23 [ecd8]   - WHAT do they like to do?
18:56:23 [ecd8]   - WHO sees some flowers?
18:56:23 [ecd8]   - WHAT does she want to do with the flowers?
18:56:23 [ecd8]   - WHERE does the cow find an opening?
18:56:23 [ecd8]   - WHERE are the flowers?
18:56:23 [ecd8] 
18:56:23 [ecd8] Example 2 (setting: backyard playground):
18:56:23 [ecd8]   Story: Today is Saturday. The children are playing in the backyard. A boy named Blake goes down the slide. Next he will play on the swings. A boy named Jack is too tired to play. He finds a blanket and takes a nap on the blanket.
18:56:23 [ecd8]   Questions:
18:56:23 [ecd8]   - WHAT day is it?
18:56:23 [ecd8]   - WHERE are the children playing?
18:56:23 [ecd8]   - WHO is going down the slide?
18:56:23 [ecd8]   - WHAT will Blake do next?
18:56:23 [ecd8]   - WHO is too tired to play?
18:56:23 [ecd8]   - WHAT does Jack do?
18:56:23 [ecd8]   - WHERE does he sleep?
18:56:23 [ecd8] 
18:56:23 [ecd8] --- END STYLE REFERENCE ---
18:56:23 [ecd8] 
18:56:23 [ecd8] --- AGE-APPROPRIATE LANGUAGE REQUIREMENTS ---
18:56:23 [ecd8] Language level: Write concrete sentences in the present tense. Use simple vocabulary a 4–5 year old already knows (ball, beach, dog, bus, kite, snow, library). Each sentence states a single observable fact (who is there, where, what is happening, what surprises them). Avoid abstract concepts, idioms, figurative language, and complex compound sentences. Story structure: One small scene — everyday or imaginative — with a tiny twist or surprise. Examples of scenes: building a sandcastle and finding a crab; flying a kite and the wind blowing it onto a dog; dropping toothpaste on the bathroom floor; spotting an unusual lunch in the cafeteria. Characters: 1–3 named characters; refer to them by name (not pronouns) so questions are answerable. Follow-up questions: After the story, generate 5–7 WH-questions (WHO/WHAT/WHERE ONLY — no HOW/WHY at this age) whose answers are explicitly stated in the story text — one fact per question, in roughly story order.
18:56:23 [ecd8] 
18:56:23 [ecd8] --- STORY SETTING AND THEME ---
18:56:23 [ecd8] Set the story in a familiar, child-friendly environment.
18:56:23 [ecd8] The obstacle should involve a manageable challenge that requires help from others.
18:56:23 [ecd8] The resolution should leave the protagonist feeling proud, grateful, and connected.
18:56:23 [ecd8] 
18:56:23 [ecd8] Therapy goals to integrate naturally into the story (do NOT list them explicitly — weave them into narrative, dialogue, and action):
18:56:23 [ecd8] 
18:56:23 [ecd8] - Learning descriptive words (adjectives, spatial terms)
18:56:23 [ecd8] - Collaboration and turn-taking
18:56:23 [ecd8] - Importance of friendships and relationships
18:56:23 [ecd8] - Overcoming challenges with support
18:56:23 [ecd8] 
