19:37:18 [b46a] - taxonomic thematic relation: Ask how two items are related by category or by everyday use/event context. e.g. How are apples and bananas alike?
19:37:18 [b46a] - cross classification: Ask whether one item can be classified in multiple valid ways. e.g. Why can an apple be both a fruit and a snack?
19:37:18 [b46a] - superordinate subordinate reasoning: Ask whether an item is a kind of another category, or whether one category is broader than another. e.g. What kind of animal is a poodle?
19:37:18 [b46a] - multi attribute categorization: Ask the child to reason using two or more features. e.g. Why do umbrellas help in rain?
19:37:18 [b46a] - origin and processing reasoning: Ask about natural source, animal source, ingredient source, or simple processing pathway. e.g. How are oranges changed into orange juice?
19:37:18 [b46a] - ingredient to food pathway: Ask simple ingredient-to-product relationships. e.g. What is bread made from?
19:37:18 [b46a] - causal explanation: Ask why something happens or why an object/action is useful. e.g. Why do people use umbrellas when it rains?
19:37:18 [b46a] - problem solution reasoning: Ask what someone should do when a familiar problem occurs. e.g. What can you do if a pencil breaks?
19:37:18 [b46a] - narrative goal attempt outcome: Ask about what a character wanted, what they did, what happened, and how they felt. e.g. What did the child want?
19:37:18 [b46a] - prediction inference: Ask the child to make a simple prediction or inference from a familiar context. e.g. What might happen when clouds get dark?
19:37:18 [b46a] - event sequence and planning: Ask whether an action happens before, after, or to prepare for a familiar event. e.g. What do we do to our hands before eating?
