21:57:23 [e71b] [StoryGenerator] prompt:  Write a short story for a 5-year-old female named sophie. The story should be developmentally appropriate, engaging, and supportive of language comprehension and literacy development.
21:57:23 [e71b] 
21:57:23 [e71b] --- STYLE REFERENCE (from the curated 4-to-7 corpus, WHO/WHAT/WHERE subset for this age) ---
21:57:23 [e71b] Follow the general style, length, vocabulary, and structure of these examples. Each story is followed by 5–7 WH-questions whose answers are explicitly stated in the story.
21:57:23 [e71b] Example 1 (setting: backyard playground):
21:57:23 [e71b]   Story: Today is Saturday. The children are playing in the backyard. A boy named Blake goes down the slide. Next he will play on the swings. A boy named Jack is too tired to play. He finds a blanket and takes a nap on the blanket.
21:57:23 [e71b]   Questions:
21:57:23 [e71b]   - WHAT day is it?
21:57:23 [e71b]   - WHERE are the children playing?
21:57:23 [e71b]   - WHO is going down the slide?
21:57:23 [e71b]   - WHAT will Blake do next?
21:57:23 [e71b]   - WHO is too tired to play?
21:57:23 [e71b]   - WHAT does Jack do?
21:57:23 [e71b]   - WHERE does he sleep?
21:57:23 [e71b] 
21:57:23 [e71b] Example 2 (setting: cafeteria):
21:57:23 [e71b]   Story: Rosie goes to the cafeteria. It is time for lunch. Rosie looks for a seat. She sees her friends' lunches on the table. Rosie sees a whole pineapple! She wonders who brought a whole pineapple for lunch!
21:57:23 [e71b]   Questions:
21:57:23 [e71b]   - WHERE does Rosie go?
21:57:23 [e71b]   - WHAT time is it?
21:57:23 [e71b]   - WHO looks for a seat?
21:57:23 [e71b]   - WHERE are her friends' lunches?
21:57:23 [e71b]   - WHO sees a whole pineapple?
21:57:23 [e71b]   - WHAT does she wonder?
21:57:23 [e71b] 
21:57:23 [e71b] --- END STYLE REFERENCE ---
21:57:23 [e71b] 
21:57:23 [e71b] --- AGE-APPROPRIATE LANGUAGE REQUIREMENTS ---
21:57:23 [e71b] Language level: Write concrete sentences in the present tense. Use simple vocabulary a 4–5 year old already knows (ball, beach, dog, bus, kite, snow, library). Each sentence states a single observable fact (who is there, where, what is happening, what surprises them). Avoid abstract concepts, idioms, figurative language, and complex compound sentences. Story structure: One small scene — everyday or imaginative — with a tiny twist or surprise. Examples of scenes: building a sandcastle and finding a crab; flying a kite and the wind blowing it onto a dog; dropping toothpaste on the bathroom floor; spotting an unusual lunch in the cafeteria. Characters: 1–3 named characters; refer to them by name (not pronouns) so questions are answerable. Follow-up questions: After the story, generate 5–7 WH-questions (WHO/WHAT/WHERE ONLY — no HOW/WHY at this age) whose answers are explicitly stated in the story text — one fact per question, in roughly story order.
21:57:23 [e71b] 
21:57:23 [e71b] --- STORY SETTING AND THEME ---
21:57:23 [e71b] Set the story in a familiar, child-friendly environment.
21:57:23 [e71b] The obstacle should involve a manageable challenge that requires help from others.
21:57:23 [e71b] The resolution should leave the protagonist feeling proud, grateful, and connected.
21:57:23 [e71b] 
21:57:23 [e71b] Therapy goals to integrate naturally into the story (do NOT list them explicitly — weave them into narrative, dialogue, and action):
21:57:23 [e71b] 
21:57:23 [e71b] - Learning descriptive words (adjectives, spatial terms)
21:57:23 [e71b] - Collaboration and turn-taking
21:57:23 [e71b] - Importance of friendships and relationships
21:57:23 [e71b] - Overcoming challenges with support
21:57:23 [e71b] 
21:57:23 [e71b] --- LANGUAGE & INTEREST GUIDANCE (knowledge base) ---
21:57:23 [e71b] Target developmental level: age 5, approx MLU 6-8 words per utterance. Keep sentences at or near this length.
21:57:23 [e71b] 
21:57:23 [e71b] Weave these language targets naturally into narration and dialogue (model them in context; do not drill or quiz them in the story):
21:57:23 [e71b] - plural forms (Regular plural nouns): e.g. dogs, cars, apples, cats, toys
21:57:23 [e71b] - ing form (Present participle/progressive verb form): e.g. running, jumping, eating, playing
21:57:23 [e71b] - pronouns later (Later developing pronouns: possessive and plural third-person forms): e.g. her, his, they, their
21:57:23 [e71b] - location noun (Location concept expressed with a noun phrase): e.g. in the box, on the table, under the chair
21:57:23 [e71b] - simple conjunction (Early conjunction use): e.g. and, or
